[Music]  in tonight's whatever happened to  segment massive reforms called for at  the National Security Agency over  surveillance on Americans the  information has also had massive  implications in Europe correspondent  Jillian Turner has our story tonight  from Great Britain one time NSA  contractor and CIA employee Edward  Snowden is believed to be in Hong Kong  tonight hiding out after leaking  information the Snowden revelations  shook the world during the summer of  2013 and ever since the National  Security Agency and US Congress have  been embroiled in an epic battle over  the legality of mass surveillance back  then the 29 year old NSA contractor  publicly confessed to stealing a trove  of highly classified intelligence about  US surveillance programs Snowden handed  that Intel over wholesale to the UK's  Guardian newspaper and New York Times  and in the process revealed that the  Intel community was collecting  information about Americans suspected of  having ties to terrorism prompting a  national outcry what Snowden did more  than anything else is he brought one  word to the forefront and that was  privacy get burst the bubble people  thought they had far more privacy than  their really good a lots happened in the  ensuing years since the very public  fallout members of Congress insists  they've used their oversight authority  to tamp down on NSA operations most of  which I can't go into chastened the  nation's leading Intel officials I would  follow the law and I would ensure if  confirmed that the agency follows the  law Congress and the Defense Department  also established US Cyber Command to  provide military support for u.s.  networks and while Intel experts widely  view these steps is effective they also  acknowledge the NSA continues data  collection of Americans about 530  million calls in just a few years the  problem is we're collecting more and  more but the real question is how much  of this are we really processing how  much this is really turning into things  that we can make decisions on European  countries were also hit hard by the  Snowden revelations Britain's government  in particular is still grappling to this  day with major fallout as the u.s.  restricted government access to  America's data Britain's Parliament went  in the opposite direction it passed  legislation known as the snoopers  charter in November 2016 it requires  internet companies to keep logs of  customer browsing data for one year the  law is so contentious here in Britain  it's been fought over in the High Court  virtually since the day it passed and  just last month the court ruled it  violates British citizen's right to  privacy and ordered the government to  draft new legislation by November 1st  Brett Joanne Turner in London Jillian  thank you

